---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const reflowCss = `.responsive-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(
    auto-fit,
    minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr)
  );
  gap: var(--space-inline-md);
}

.responsive-grid > * {
  min-inline-size: 0;
}`;

const mediaCss = `.content {
  inline-size: min(100%, 70rem);
}

.content img,
.content video {
  max-inline-size: 100%;
  block-size: auto;
}

/* Avoid fixed heights: enlarged text needs to grow. */
.card {
  min-block-size: 12rem;
  block-size: auto;
}`;

const localScrollCss = `.table-scroll,
.code-scroll {
  max-inline-size: 100%;
  overflow-x: auto;
  overscroll-behavior-inline: contain;
}`;

const auditTs = `import { expect, test } from "@playwright/test";

const routes = ["/in", "/settings", "/checkout"];
const widths = [640, 320];

for (const route of routes) {
  for (const width of widths) {
    test(\`\${route} reflows at \${width}px\`, async ({ page }) => {
      await page.setViewportSize({ width, height: 900 });
      await page.goto(route);

      const hasPageOverflow = await page.evaluate(() => {
        const root = document.documentElement;
        return root.scrollWidth > root.clientWidth;
      });

      expect(hasPageOverflow).toBe(false);
      await expect(page.locator("main")).toBeVisible();
    });
  }
}`;
---

<Base
  title="Zoom y reflow"
  description="Text at 200% and reflow at 400% without loss of content, controls or functionality."
>
  <h1>Zoom y reflow</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Zoom is not a visual preference or media query. It is a transformation of the viewport and the
    readable size: content should wrap, columns should stack, and all controls
    They must remain available. The interface changes geometry, not capabilities.
  </p>

  <div class="sk-alert" data-tone="info" data-presentation="accent">
    <div class="sk-alert__content">
      <p>
        <strong>200% and 400% prove different contracts.</strong> At 200% it is verified that the text
        can grow without losing content or functionality. At 400% reflow is checked: a viewport of
        1280px is equivalent to <strong>320 CSS px</strong> and the page must be readable in one
        direction.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Dos pruebas, dos fallos posibles</h2>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Prueba</th>
          <th>What does it require?</th>
          <th>Typical fault</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>200%</strong></td>
          <td>Readable text and operable controls</td>
          <td>Labels cut by fixed heights</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>400%</strong></td>
          <td>Reflow a 320 CSS px</td>
          <td>Columns or toolbars outside the viewport</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The layout retains capacity</h2>
  <p>
    This sample uses the same rule as a real card list. In wide width it has three
    columns; in the equivalent of 400% it converges into one. No card disappears or changes
    order.
  </p>

  <figure class="reflow-specimen">
    <div class="reflow-specimen__grid">
      <article class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="md">
        <span class="reflow-specimen__index">01</span>
        <strong>Content</strong>
        <p>The text wraps and grows its own box.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="md">
        <span class="reflow-specimen__index">02</span>
        <strong>Controles</strong>
        <p>Actions remain visible and reachable.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="sk-box" data-surface="surface" data-border="subtle" data-padding="md">
        <span class="reflow-specimen__index">03</span>
        <strong>Orden</strong>
        <p>The DOM maintains a coherent reading sequence.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <figcaption>
      The grid does not need a breakpoint per device: <code>auto-fit</code> responds to space
      available and <code>min(100%, 18rem)</code> prevents a column from imposing overflow.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <CodeBlock code={reflowCss} label="CSS · grid with reflow" />

  <h2>Implementation rules</h2>

  <h3>Let the text grow and a half</h3>
  <p>
    Avoid fixed heights in boxes with contents. Images and video are limited to the available width without
    deform its proportion.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={mediaCss} label="CSS · flexible content" />

  <h3>Horizontal scrolling is local, not global</h3>
  <p>
    Data tables, code, maps and diagrams may require two dimensions. The scroll belongs
    to that component; the title, the explanation and the rest of the page continue to reflow.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={localScrollCss} label="CSS · overflow local" />

  <h2>How to guarantee it in your system</h2>

  <h3>1. Make the container decide</h3>
  <p>
    A component should not know the width of the device. Give it a flexible width, allow
    its children shrink with <code>min-inline-size: 0</code> and let the container change columns,
    wrapping or distribution according to the real space.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Define a reflow contract per component</h3>
  <div class="sk-table-scroll">
    <table class="sk-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Tipo</th>
          <th>Minimum contract</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Text, Alert, Tile, Card</td>
          <td>automatic height; labels and content can wrap.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Navbar y toolbar</td>
          <td>Actions wrap, change rows or move to an accessible menu.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Dialog, Drawer y popover</td>
          <td>They fit into the viewport; The bodysuit has internal scrolling if necessary.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Grid y Stack</td>
          <td>The columns converge without changing the order of the DOM.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Table, code, map</td>
          <td>The horizontal overflow is contained in the component.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Audit all routes</h3>
  <p>
    Runs an array with each path and the effective widths of 200% and 400%. This test detects
    global overflow automatically; adds to each flow its essential interactions because a
    Visible interface may still have lost functionality.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={auditTs} label="Playwright reflow matrix" lang="ts" />

  <h3>4. End with real zoom</h3>
  <p>
    An automated narrow viewport tests the geometry, but is not a substitute for final review:
    Open the browser at 200% and 400%, scroll through the page with the keyboard and try menus, dialogs,
    tooltips, validations and states with long content.
  </p>

  <h2>What breaks with zoom</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Fixed <code>width</code> or <code>min-width</code> greater than the effective viewport.</li>
    <li><code>height</code> fixed on buttons, cards, alerts or fields with text.</li>
    <li><code>white-space: nowrap</code> on labels that do not have a responsive output.</li>
    <li>Toolbars that hide actions instead of wrapping or rearranging them.</li>
    <li>Content positioned with physical offsets that stops participating in the flow.</li>
    <li><code>overflow: hidden</code> used to hide the symptom instead of fixing the width.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Documentation Site Contract</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      At 200%, the effective 640 px CSS layout does not produce global horizontal scrolling.
    </li>
    <li>
      At 400%, the effective 320 px CSS layout stacks the content in one column.
    </li>
    <li>
      The header goes to two rows: identity on top; search and preferences below.
    </li>
    <li>
      Menu, search, palette, theme and contrast remain visible and operable.
    </li>
    <li>
      Code blocks and tables contain their own overflow when they need it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Test 200% text, not just a mobile viewport.</li>
    <li>Try 400% from 1280px or directly to 320 CSS px.</li>
    <li>Read the entire page using only vertical scrolling.</li>
    <li>Open menus, dialogs and popovers; visible does not mean operable.</li>
    <li>Change theme and preferences to detect overlapping controls.</li>
    <li>Confirm that focus and reading order follow the visual order.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Referencias:
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/resize-text.html">
      WCAG 1.4.4 Resize Text
    </a>
    y
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/reflow.html">
      WCAG 1.4.10 Reflow
    </a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .reflow-specimen {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-stack-sm);
    margin: var(--space-stack-md) 0 var(--space-section);
  }

  .reflow-specimen__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(
      auto-fit,
      minmax(min(100%, 12rem), 1fr)
    );
    gap: var(--space-inline-md);
    padding: var(--space-inset-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-surface);
    background: var(--color-bg-surface-sunken);
  }

  .reflow-specimen__grid article {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-stack-xs);
    min-inline-size: 0;
    align-content: start;
  }

  .reflow-specimen__grid p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .reflow-specimen__index {
    color: var(--color-text-accent);
    font-family: var(--font-family-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .reflow-specimen figcaption {
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
    line-height: var(--font-line-height-caption);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
</style>
